Meridian University Financial and Academic Administration Regulations

Document ID: MU-RULEBOOK-2026-ADMIN
Version: 2026.1
Effective Date: July 1, 2026
Issuing Authority: Office of Academic Administration

26. Tuition and Academic Fees
26.1 Fee Schedule

The university publishes an official fee schedule before the beginning of each academic year.

The fee schedule identifies tuition fees, examination fees, laboratory charges, hostel charges, library charges, and other applicable administrative fees.

Students are responsible for reviewing the fee schedule applicable to their programme.

26.2 Payment Deadline

Fees must be paid by the deadline specified in the official fee schedule or subsequent university notice.

A student who fails to pay an applicable fee by the deadline may become subject to a late-payment charge or administrative restriction.

26.3 Payment Methods

The university may provide electronic payment, bank transfer, or other approved payment methods.

Students should retain evidence of payment until the payment has been reflected in their official account.

26.4 Failed Transactions

Where a payment transaction fails but the student's account is debited, the student should report the issue to the designated finance office.

The university may request transaction details or other evidence before investigating the matter.

27. Examination Fees
27.1 Examination Fee Requirement

Students must pay the applicable examination fee within the examination registration period when an examination fee is prescribed.

The examination office may refuse to process an incomplete examination registration where the applicable fee has not been paid.

27.2 Fee Payment and Eligibility

Payment of an examination fee does not override academic eligibility requirements.

Students must satisfy attendance, assessment, registration, and other applicable requirements independently.

27.3 Refunds

Examination fees may be refunded only where the applicable university policy permits a refund.

A refund request should be submitted through the designated process.

27.4 Duplicate Payments

Where a student has accidentally made a duplicate payment, the student should notify the finance office and provide evidence of both transactions.

The finance office may verify the transactions before processing an adjustment or refund.

28. Scholarships and Financial Assistance
28.1 Scholarship Categories

The university may provide scholarships or financial assistance based on academic performance, financial need, achievement, participation, or other criteria established by the relevant scholarship programme.

Each scholarship programme may specify separate eligibility, continuation, and renewal requirements.

28.2 Application

Students must submit scholarship applications within the published application period.

Applications submitted after the deadline may be rejected unless the scholarship rules expressly permit late applications.

28.3 Academic Requirements

A scholarship programme may require students to maintain a specified academic performance level.

The applicable scholarship notice must state the academic requirements for the relevant scholarship.

28.4 Attendance Requirement

A scholarship programme may impose an attendance requirement when that requirement is explicitly stated in its published terms.

Where a scholarship does not specify a separate attendance requirement, the university's general attendance regulations continue to apply.

28.5 Continuation

Continuation of a scholarship is subject to satisfaction of the requirements specified by the applicable scholarship programme.

Failure to satisfy a continuation requirement may result in suspension or termination of the scholarship.

28.6 Review of Scholarship Decisions

A student who believes that a scholarship decision was made using incorrect information may request a review where the applicable scholarship programme provides a review mechanism.

The review request should identify the relevant decision and provide supporting evidence.

29. Academic Progress
29.1 Progress Monitoring

The university may monitor student academic progress using course results, attendance records, assessment performance, and other relevant academic information.

29.2 Academic Warning

A student whose academic progress falls below a programme-defined standard may receive an academic warning.

The warning should identify the relevant academic concern and, where appropriate, provide information about available academic support.

29.3 Academic Support

Students receiving an academic warning may be encouraged to meet with an academic advisor or programme coordinator.

Available support may include tutoring, academic advising, study planning, or other university-approved support.

29.4 Continued Poor Performance

Where a student continues to fail to satisfy academic progress requirements, the university may apply academic consequences authorized by the applicable programme or university regulations.

30. Course Registration
30.1 Registration Period

Students must register for courses during the registration period announced by the university.

The registration system may restrict registration when prerequisites, programme requirements, or administrative conditions have not been satisfied.

30.2 Prerequisites

A student should not normally register for a course when a required prerequisite has not been successfully completed.

Exceptions may be granted only by the authority authorized under the applicable programme regulations.

30.3 Registration Changes

Students may add or drop courses only during the period specified by the academic calendar or applicable registration notice.

After the deadline, changes require approval where the university permits such changes.

30.4 Course Withdrawal

Where permitted, a student may withdraw from a registered course before the applicable withdrawal deadline.

A withdrawal may be recorded on the student's academic record according to university policy.

31. Academic Records and Transcripts
31.1 Grade Records

Course grades must be recorded through the university's authorized academic records system.

Only authorized personnel may modify official grade records.

31.2 Grade Corrections

A student who believes that a recorded grade contains an error should contact the relevant instructor or academic office through the prescribed process.

The university may require evidence supporting the requested correction.

31.3 Transcript Requests

Students may request official transcripts through the designated academic records process.

The university may require payment of an applicable transcript fee.

31.4 Confidentiality

Official academic records should be disclosed only through authorized procedures and to persons or organizations entitled to receive them under applicable requirements.

32. Academic Appeals
32.1 General Appeal

Where an academic decision is subject to appeal, the affected student may submit an appeal within the deadline specified by the applicable procedure.

32.2 Appeal Contents

An academic appeal should clearly identify:

the decision being appealed;
the grounds for appeal;
relevant evidence; and
the remedy or review requested.
32.3 Supporting Evidence

Students should provide relevant documents or information supporting their appeal.

The reviewing authority may request additional information where necessary.

32.4 Appeal Outcome

The reviewing authority may uphold, modify, or overturn the decision where permitted by the applicable regulations.

The outcome should be communicated through an official university channel.

33. Administrative Authority
33.1 Academic Departments

Academic departments are responsible for implementing applicable academic policies within their programmes.

Departments may issue procedural instructions where those instructions are consistent with university regulations.

33.2 Examination Office

The examination office administers examination registration, examination schedules, eligibility lists, examination procedures, and related administrative processes.

33.3 Finance Office

The finance office administers tuition payments, examination fees, refunds, payment records, and other financial processes assigned to it.

33.4 Student Affairs Office

The student affairs office coordinates student support, leave procedures, student welfare processes, and other responsibilities assigned under university regulations.

34. Policy Interpretation
34.1 Interpretation Authority

Questions concerning the interpretation of a university-wide academic regulation may be referred to the Office of Academic Affairs.

34.2 Conflicting Administrative Instructions

Where an informal instruction appears inconsistent with a published university regulation, the published regulation should be referred to the appropriate authority for clarification.

Students should seek formal clarification rather than relying solely on informal instructions.

34.3 Policy Updates

The university may amend regulations through the appropriate approval process.

An amended regulation should identify its effective date.

End of Part 4

